==============================================================================
  2. Provenance of the winners, from inside the language
==============================================================================
  `why` and `generation` are LOVA operators now; each line below
  was written by the program about its own winner.
  seed 0: gen  6  mutate strength=0.3 [lit:12->15]
  seed 1: gen  5  mutate strength=0.3 [lit:20->25]
  seed 2: gen  3  mutate strength=0.3 [lit:14->19,lit:21->18]
  seed 3: gen  1  mutate strength=0.3 [lit:10->12]
  variants that trapped under the scorer across all runs: 0  (scored UNFIT, recorded in rt.caught, never crashed a pool)

==============================================================================
  3. What differs from Exp 05
==============================================================================
  Exp 05: strength 0.45, 12 rounds x 10 dispatches, Python-driven,
          best seed 35 -> 1 (97% from worst), 3/10 within +-2,
          mean 80% closed from worst.
  Here:   strength 0.30 (the library default `evolve` encodes),
          30 generations x 1 refill, LOVA-driven,
          mean 85% closed from worst, 3/10 within +-2.
  Same rule, different setting -- not the same run.  The point
  of this experiment is not the number; it is that the number
  was produced by a LOVA program calling `evolve`, `select`,
  `generation` and `why`, with Python doing nothing but seeding
  and printing.  Axiom 6 is in the language.

==============================================================================
  Experiment 15 -- summary
==============================================================================
  9/10 seeds improve, 3/10 converge, mean 85% of the worst-case gap closed, all from
  inside LOVA; the Evolution family is 8/8 implemented.
